==English==
{{was wotd|2019|April|27}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|LL.|recrudescentia}}, from {{der|en|la|recrūdēscēns}}, {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la|recrūdēscere||to recrudesce}}, from {{m|la|recrūdēscō||to become raw again}}; from {{m|la|re-||''prefix meaning ‘again’''}} + {{m|la|crūdēscō||to grow harsh or violent; to become worsen}} (from {{m|la|crūdus||bleeding, bloody, raw}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*krewh₂-||blood outside the body}}) + {{m|la|-ēscō||''suffix forming verbs indicating a becoming of something''}}). The word is cognate with {{cog|fr|recrudescence}}, {{cog|it|recrudescenza}}, {{cog|es|recrudescencia}}, {{m|es|recrudecimiento}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|part of speech=n|id=159984|date=June 2009|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Oxford Dictionaries Online}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˌɹiːkɹuːˈdɛs(ə)ns/|/ˌɹɛ-/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/ˌɹikɹuˈdɛs(ə)ns/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|re|cru|des|ence|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The condition or state being [[recrudescent]]; the condition of something (often [[undesirable]]) [[break out|breaking out]] again, or [[reemerge|re-emerging]] after [[temporary]] [[abatement]] or [[suppression]]. {{defdate|from mid 17th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|recrudency|q1=archaic|reincrudation|q2=obsolete}}
#* Duke of Argyll
#*: A '''recrudescence''' of barbarism may condemn it [land] to chronic poverty and waste.
# {{lb|en|medicine|by extension}} The [[acute#Adjective|acute]] [[recurrence]] of a [[disease]], or its [[symptom]]s, after a period of [[improvement]].
# {{lb|en|botany}} The [[production]] of a [[fresh]] [[shoot#Noun|shoot]] from a [[ripened#Adjective|ripened]] [[spike#Noun|spike]].

